# Test budget
A suite that was made fast stays fast only if every new test follows the same
rules. A few new tests with a sleep, a real HTTP call, or a leaked timer make the
suite slow and flaky again, and one slow new file can become the whole suite's
long pole. The cheapest moment to prevent this is when the test is written.
## Design checklist
| Question | Good | Bottleneck |
|---|---|---|
| What level? | Unit test of the function or component; integration only for wiring | End-to-end test for logic a unit test could check |
| Does it depend on time? | Fake timers, an injected clock, or a configurable interval | A sleep, or waiting out a real interval |
| Does it test a failure? | A non-retried error, or zero retry delays injected | A thrown network error that the client retries with backoff |
| What does it touch? | Temp dir, port 0, its own schema, in-memory fakes | Fixed paths, fixed ports, a shared database, real services |
| What does it leave behind? | Nothing: servers closed, timers cleared, threads joined | Background timers or sockets that hold the process open |
| What does it assert? | Expected behavior from the specification; fails when the fix is reverted | "No exception", or a snapshot of current output |
| Where does it live? | The feature's test file, kept small | Appended to the largest, slowest file |
A new end-to-end test needs a stated reason. Language-specific fixes for time,
retries, and open handles are in the verify skill's test waits reference.
## Budget check for new and changed tests
Run the scanner and the profiler on the test files this change adds or modifies:
BASE=${BASE:-origin/main}
FILES=$(git diff --name-only --diff-filter=AM "$BASE"...HEAD | grep -E '(^|/)(test|tests|spec|__tests__)/|[._](test|spec)\.' || true)
[ -z "$FILES" ] && exit 0
node ${JSTACK_PLUGIN_ROOT}/skills/verify/scripts/test_smells.js $FILES
node ${JSTACK_PLUGIN_ROOT}/skills/verify/scripts/test_profile.js --cmd "npx vitest run {file}" --max-seconds 2 --fail-on-waiting --repeat 5 $FILES
- `--max-seconds` is the per-file budget: unit files 1 to 2 s, integration files
up to 10 s, end-to-end set separately. A new file must never become the suite's
current long pole.
- `--fail-on-waiting` fails a file that mostly waits even under budget; that wait
will grow.
- `--repeat 5` runs each file five times; differing outcomes are reported as
FLAKY. For stronger checks, add random order inside `--cmd` (`--randomize`,
`-shuffle=on`, `pytest-randomly`) and run once with more workers than cores.
- A WAITING, OVER, or FLAKY result is fixed in the test or in the code's timers,
never by raising the budget. The profiler exits 1 on any violation, so the same
command works as a CI step or a pre-push hook.
- For Go, the unit is a package: pass `$(dirname $FILES | sort -u)` with
`--cmd "go test ./{file}"`.
## Enforce it in CI
A GitHub Actions job for a JavaScript project (adapt the runner command). Keep the
complete suite as a separate required check; this job only guards what the pull
request adds.
name: new-tests-budget
on: pull_request
jobs:
budget:
runs-on: ubuntu-latest
steps:
- uses: actions/checkout@v4
with: { fetch-depth: 0 }
- uses: actions/setup-node@v4
with: { node-version: 22 }
- run: npm ci
- name: Budget for new and changed test files
run: |
FILES=$(git diff --name-only --diff-filter=AM origin/${{ github.base_ref }}...HEAD \
| grep -E '(^|/)(test|tests|spec|__tests__)/|[._](test|spec)\.' || true)
[ -z "$FILES" ] && exit 0
node "$JSTACK_PLUGIN_ROOT/skills/verify/scripts/test_smells.js" $FILES
node "$JSTACK_PLUGIN_ROOT/skills/verify/scripts/test_profile.js" --cmd "npx vitest run {file}" \
--max-seconds 2 --fail-on-waiting --repeat 5 $FILES
Copy the two scripts into the repository, or point `JSTACK_PLUGIN_ROOT` at an
installed jstack payload, when CI has no plugin installation.
## A guard for structural fixes
When a structural cause is fixed (a timer that held processes open, a retry that
tests triggered), add a test that fails if it returns. For example, a fixture that
leaves 30 s timers behind must exit within 10 s, run in a child process with a
timeout. A change that reintroduces the problem then fails in seconds instead of
silently adding minutes.
## Review a test someone else wrote
Agent-written tests often have weak or no assertions. Before accepting one:
- Does each test assert the expected behavior from the specification or issue,
not merely "no exception"?
- Would it fail if the fix were reverted? Revert the source change and run it, or
prove it with `node ${JSTACK_PLUGIN_ROOT}/skills/verify/scripts/red_probe.js`.
- Are error paths covered, not only the happy path?
- Is it deterministic (stress it 20 times in random order) and fast (no waits)?
Never make a test pass by deleting, skipping, special-casing, or loosening it
unless the task is to change that test. If a test contradicts the
specification, say so and ask.
## Order of work for a new test
1. Write it from the specification or bug report; run it and watch it fail for
the right reason.
2. Change the code; run the test and the affected tests.
3. Run the budget check on the files added or changed, and fix any WAITING, OVER,
or FLAKY result in the test.
4. Report the budget check and the affected-check summary; the required gate runs
the complete suite.
5. Leave the suite at least as fast as you found it: no new sleeps, fixed ports,
fixed paths, or real network calls.
